  s  14736ee6259b7778ca2ce82ae3412432e280cf93235dccebe801f26f4119a9d6

Signing is RFC6979-deterministic, so re-running prove.py with the same challenge reproduces these
exact values. Low-s normalised. The digest convention is Bitcoin's own: double-SHA256 of the ASCII
hex challenge.

  reproduce   python ../verify/build_chain_source_manifest.py
              python prove.py "<the manifest sha256 it prints>"


WHAT THIS BINDS, AND WHY IT WAS THE LAST ONE
--------------------------------------------

This laboratory measures whether four identities are cryptographically bound -- the whitepaper
author line (W), the source copyright line (L), the chain keys (K) and the OpenPGP key (P). For
Satoshi Nakamoto's 2008-2009 record, every one of the six pairs is unbound: ZERO OF SIX, measured.

Run against this project on 12 August 2026, five of six were bound. The exception was L <-> K, and
its status was "by construction": the source carrying the copyright line is the source that mined
our genesis, but nobody had signed anything to that effect.

That is precisely the standard this laboratory refuses to accept from anybody else. Satoshi's own
L <-> K is "by construction" in exactly the same sense -- the client carrying the copyright line is
the client that mined block 0 -- and we call that administrative, not cryptographic, because it is.

  BEFORE   the source built the chain, and the connection was an argument about causation
  AFTER    the key that mined block 0 has SIGNED a manifest of the source that built block 0

The manifest covers 45 files: the 43-file client source tree, of which 29 still carry
"Copyright (c) 2009 Satoshi Nakamoto" with the MIT/X11 notice intact; PROVENANCE.txt, which states
what is Satoshi's, what is Wei Dai's and what is ours; and bitcoin-v0.1.0.patch, the ten lines that
separate this chain from Satoshi's.

Build outputs are deliberately excluded. They are products of the source rather than the source,
and they are not byte-reproducible across toolchains -- a manifest that fails for an honest
verifier on a different machine would be worse than one that does not cover them.


WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
-------------------

  DOES NOT   claim authorship of Satoshi's 2009 code. The copyright line is retained BECAUSE it is
             his. This signature attests to what the tree contains, not to who wrote it.

  DOES NOT   prove who operates this key, or that any person is any other person. A signature
             binds keys to bytes. It has never bound a key to a person, here or anywhere.

  DOES NOT   assert any right in the name "Bitcoin" or the name "Satoshi Nakamoto", make this
             chain money, or create any claim on the 2009 chain. No premine, no token, no sale,
             no price, no offer.

  DOES NOT   change the source. Re-running the builder on an unchanged tree reproduces the
             manifest byte-for-byte, so this signature stays valid until the source itself moves.
             When it moves, the manifest must be rebuilt and re-signed -- and the old signature
             will correctly stop verifying, which is the point.
